If you need to incorporate this logic into your React component, you can define the transformation within the component. Here's a full example:

```jsx
import React, { useState } from 'react';

const FruitForm = () => {
  const criteria = {
    name: { value: 1, editable: false },
    description: { value: 0, editable: true }
  };

  const searchOptions = Object.keys(criteria).reduce((acc, key) => {
    acc[key] = criteria[key].value;
    return acc;
  }, {});

  console.log('Search Options:', searchOptions);

  const [fruits, setFruits] = useState(criteria);

  const handleChange = (event) => {
    const { name, checked } = event.target;
    setFruits((prevFruits) => ({
      ...prevFruits,
      [name]: { ...prevFruits[name], value: checked ? 1 : 0 }
    }));
  };

  const handleSubmit = (event) => {
    event.preventDefault();
    const selectedOptions = Object.keys(fruits).reduce((acc, key) => {
      acc[key] = fruits[key].value;
      return acc;
    }, {});
    console.log('Selected Fruits:', selectedOptions);
  };

  return (
    <form onSubmit={handleSubmit}>
      <div style={{ display: "flex", alignItems: "center", padding: "10px" }}>
        <label htmlFor="searchOptions" style={{ width: "9%", fontWeight: "bold" }}>Search Options</label>
        {Object.keys(fruits).map((fruit) => (
          <div key={fruit}>
            <label>
              <input
                type="checkbox"
                name={fruit}
                checked={fruits[fruit].value === 1}
                onChange={handleChange}
                disabled={!fruits[fruit].editable}
              />
              {fruit.charAt(0).toUpperCase() + fruit.slice(1)}
            </label>
          </div>
        ))}
      </div>
      <button type="submit">Submit</button>
    </form>
  );
};

export default FruitForm;
```

In this code:

1. The `criteria` object is defined with the required structure.
2. The `searchOptions` object is derived from `criteria` using `reduce`.
3. The `fruits` state is initialized with the `criteria` object.
4. The `handleSubmit` function creates `selectedOptions` from the `fruits` state and logs it to the console.

It looks like there's a misunderstanding with the `readOnly` attribute. The `readOnly` attribute should be set to the opposite of the `editable` value. If `editable` is `false`, the checkbox should be `readOnly`, meaning the user cannot change it.

Here's the corrected code snippet:

```jsx
import React, { useState } from 'react';

const FruitForm = () => {
  const [fruits, setFruits] = useState({
    apple: { value: true, editable: false },
    banana: { value: false, editable: true },
    orange: { value: false, editable: true },
    mango: { value: false, editable: true },
    grape: { value: false, editable: true }
  });

  const handleChange = (event) => {
    const { name, checked } = event.target;
    setFruits((prevFruits) => ({
      ...prevFruits,
      [name]: { ...prevFruits[name], value: checked }
    }));
  };

  const handleSubmit = (event) => {
    event.preventDefault();
    console.log('Selected Fruits:', fruits);
  };

  return (
    <form onSubmit={handleSubmit}>
      <div style={{ display: "flex", alignItems: "center", padding: "10px" }}>
        <label htmlFor="searchOptions" style={{ width: "9%", fontWeight: "bold" }}>Search Options</label>
        {Object.keys(fruits).map((fruit) => (
          <div key={fruit}>
            <label>
              <input
                type="checkbox"
                name={fruit}
                checked={fruits[fruit].value}
                onChange={handleChange}
                disabled={!fruits[fruit].editable}
              />
              {fruit.charAt(0).toUpperCase() + fruit.slice(1)}
            </label>
          </div>
        ))}
      </div>
      <button type="submit">Submit</button>
    </form>
  );
};

export default FruitForm;
```

In this corrected code:

1. The `disabled` attribute is used instead of `readOnly`. When `editable` is `false`, `disabled` is set to `true`, making the checkbox non-editable.
2. The `checked` attribute is correctly set to the `value` property of each fruit.

This will ensure that the fruits with `editable` set to `false` are not editable.

