Yes, the `ResponseEntity` class in Spring can be used to standardize API responses, although it does not provide a built-in standardized format out-of-the-box. By using `ResponseEntity`, you can wrap your responses in a consistent format similar to `ApiResponse`, but you'll need to create the format yourself.

Here's how you can use `ResponseEntity` to standardize your API responses:

### Step 1: Create a Standard Response Format

You can use a custom response wrapper class similar to `ApiResponse`. This class can be used with `ResponseEntity` to ensure that all responses adhere to the same structure.

**`StandardResponse.java`**

```java
package com.p.java.playground.api.array.dto;

import java.time.LocalDateTime;

/**
 * A standardized response format for API responses.
 *
 * @param <T> The type of the data being returned.
 */
public class StandardResponse<T> {

    private String status;
    private String message;
    private T data;
    private int statusCode;
    private LocalDateTime timeStamp;

    /**
     * Constructor for StandardResponse.
     *
     * @param status      The status of the response (e.g., "success", "error").
     * @param message     A message describing the response.
     * @param data        The data being returned in the response.
     * @param statusCode  The HTTP status code of the response.
     * @param timeStamp   The timestamp when the response was generated.
     */
    public StandardResponse(String status, String message, T data, int statusCode, LocalDateTime timeStamp) {
        this.status = status;
        this.message = message;
        this.data = data;
        this.statusCode = statusCode;
        this.timeStamp = timeStamp;
    }

    // Getters and setters

    public String getStatus() {
        return status;
    }

    public void setStatus(String status) {
        this.status = status;
    }

    public String getMessage() {
        return message;
    }

    public void setMessage(String message) {
        this.message = message;
    }

    public T getData() {
        return data;
    }

    public void setData(T data) {
        this.data = data;
    }

    public int getStatusCode() {
        return statusCode;
    }

    public void setStatusCode(int statusCode) {
        this.statusCode = statusCode;
    }

    public LocalDateTime getTimeStamp() {
        return timeStamp;
    }

    public void setTimeStamp(LocalDateTime timeStamp) {
        this.timeStamp = timeStamp;
    }
}
```

